WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.664
Welcome to Inside the Machine, where a training workstation lets you
experiment, make predictions, and discover what happens behind an
application.

2
00:00:08.864 --> 00:00:16.610
Level one starts with an installed program and a running instance;
no debugger, Windows experience, or virtual machine is needed.

3
00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:24.939
Your goal is to connect a few observations into a useful explanation
of a healthy application, rather than memorize disconnected names.

4
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:33.732
Atlas Notes is installed but not open: its executable is a program
on disk, containing instructions and data that can be loaded.

5
00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:40.863
The file remains when no instances are running, so finding it in a
folder does not prove that instructions are executing.

6
00:00:41.063 --> 00:00:49.004
In First Contact, inspect the file, predict what launching changes,
and watch the process list: installed is not the same as running.

7
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:59.322
Our fictional Atlas Notes supports independent instances: opening it
twice creates two processes from one executable, each with a
different live process identifier.

8
00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:05.856
Close one and observe the survivor; the executable remains, because
closing an instance does not uninstall the program.

9
00:01:06.056 --> 00:01:14.941
Real applications may reuse or create multiple processes; this model
is deliberately simple, and Windows can reuse identifiers after a
process exits.

10
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:23.876
A process holds execution context and resources, including a virtual
address space and one or more threads that can execute instructions.

11
00:01:24.076 --> 00:01:31.489
It is more than a window: processes can exist without visible
windows, and a visible application can involve several processes.

12
00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:39.936
For now, inspect identity and ownership: which process owns this
work, what belongs to it, and what does your evidence actually
establish?

13
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:49.516
Threads execute instructions inside a process: one might handle
interface work while another performs an export, both belonging to
the same process.

14
00:01:49.716 --> 00:01:57.652
A long export on the interface thread can delay input; moving
suitable work to a worker can help the interface progress.

15
00:01:57.852 --> 00:02:04.932
But another thread does not create more processor capacity;
distinguish who owns the work from when it can actually run.

16
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:12.525
On one logical processor, our model runs one thread at a time;
multiple threads can make progress by taking turns.

17
00:02:12.725 --> 00:02:20.783
A ready thread can run; a waiting thread needs an event or resource
first, so unfinished work does not necessarily mean busy
computation.

18
00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:29.926
This timeline illustrates states, not Windows scheduling policy;
later levels explore processors and dependencies, while today you
distinguish ready, running, and waiting.

19
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:38.384
A virtual address needs an address-space context: the same written
address in two processes need not refer to the same underlying data.

20
00:02:38.584 --> 00:02:46.473
In A Private Address Book, inspect separate mappings, change one
process's value, and read the other value to check your prediction.

21
00:02:46.673 --> 00:02:54.935
Do not confuse the virtual map with physical memory; deeper
questions about backing, commitment, and residency belong to the
memory level.

22
00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:03.651
Separate address spaces help isolate ordinary application memory,
but processes can still communicate or intentionally share memory
through suitable mechanisms.

23
00:03:03.851 --> 00:03:12.704
Our experiment excludes shared mappings and cross-process access, so
an unchanged second value describes these particular mappings, not
every possible interaction.

24
00:03:12.904 --> 00:03:19.923
Explain the experiment's conditions as well as its result; later,
sharing and copy-on-write will add depth to this address-space
model.

25
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:29.350
Applications can calculate in user mode, while selected operations,
such as our modeled file request, need services across the user and
kernel boundary.

26
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:37.605
Follow the request: in this service path, the same calling thread
enters kernel mode and returns with a result, without creating
another process.

27
00:03:37.805 --> 00:03:44.924
Not every application interface enters the kernel, and kernel mode
does not guarantee unrestricted access; permissions still matter.

28
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:54.399
Connect the pieces: applications request services, executive
components manage memory and input and output, and the kernel
coordinates activities including thread scheduling.

29
00:03:54.599 --> 00:04:02.652
Drivers participate in selected device paths, with hardware
underneath; explain these responsibilities using the request traces
you have already followed.

30
00:04:02.852 --> 00:04:09.917
This maps cooperation, not a universal call sequence; also
distinguish the kernel component from everything that executes in
kernel mode.

31
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:17.839
Predict, change a small model, and inspect evidence: a mistaken
prediction opens a learning opportunity rather than costing points.

32
00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:26.695
Choose observations that support your explanation, use hints if
needed, and apply the concept to a changed situation rather than
repeat buttons.

33
00:04:26.895 --> 00:04:34.934
This video is optional; investigations unlock mastery without a
timer, while deeper notes and references remain available when you
want more detail.

34
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:44.467
By the end, distinguish a program, a process, and a thread using
observations, not definitions alone, and explain why a virtual
address needs context.

35
00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:50.987
Follow a selected operating-system request and connect the ideas
into an evidence-backed description of a healthy workstation.

36
00:04:51.187 --> 00:04:59.925
This is a teaching campaign, not live Windows telemetry or a
complete emulator; start First Contact when ready, and build
understanding one discovery at a time.
